# layoutRoot & layoutAnchor

Two props control which box a layout animation is measured against.

- `layoutRoot` makes an element a **layout root**: the layout animations of its descendants resolve relative to it.
- `layoutAnchor` picks the **point on the parent** a child's relative layout projection is anchored to.

## What a layout root is

A `layout` child normally follows its nearest projecting parent. `layoutRoot` makes an element that parent for
everything inside it, so descendants animate within it rather than across the page. The root's own layout change is
instant: it jumps to its new place, and its children animate inside it.

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import { motion } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'
</script>

<motion.ul layout layoutRoot>
  <!-- tabs whose layout animations resolve relative to the list -->
</motion.ul>
```

`layoutRoot` is read when the element mounts, as in Motion. Changing it afterwards has no effect. To toggle it,
remount the element, for example with `{#key}`:

```svelte
{#key isRoot}
  <motion.ul layout={isRoot} layoutRoot={isRoot}>…</motion.ul>
{/key}
```

## Shared `layoutId` inside a root

Since Motion 14.7.1 this includes shared `layoutId` animations. When the element that previously held the
`layoutId` was **inside the root too**, the new one animates relative to the root. Take a tab bar in a centred
window: selecting a tab opens a sidebar, so the whole bar jumps left in the same update as the underline moves.
With `layoutRoot` on the list, the underline glides exactly one tab. Without it, it flies in from where the old tab
used to be on the page.

<Example isSmall exampleUrl="/examples/layout-root">
  <SidebarTabs />
</Example>

```svelte
<motion.ul layout layoutRoot>
  {#each tabs as tab (tab)}
    <li>
      <button onclick={() => (selected = tab)}>{tab}</button>
      <AnimatePresence>
        {#if tab === selected}
          <motion.div key="underline" layoutId="underline" class="underline" />
        {/if}
      </AnimatePresence>
    </li>
  {/each}
</motion.ul>
```

If the previous element was **outside** the root, the new one still animates from its page position: the root
can't describe where it came from.

## `layoutAnchor`

While a parent's layout animates, a `layout` child follows it through relative projection: its position is measured
from a point on the parent. `layoutAnchor` picks that point.

- **Default, `{ x: 0, y: 0 }`**: the parent's top-left corner. The child keeps its offset from that corner.
- **`{ x: 0.5, y: 0.5 }`**: the parent's centre. A child of a centring (for example flexbox) parent stays centred
  while the parent resizes.
- **`false`**: no relative projection. The child animates in page space on its own.

<Example isSmall exampleUrl="/examples/layout-root">
  <CenteredAnchor />
</Example>

```svelte
<motion.div layout style="display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center">
  <motion.div layout layoutAnchor={{ x: 0.5, y: 0.5 }} />
</motion.div>
```

Unlike `layoutRoot`, `layoutAnchor` follows every update.

## API Reference

| Prop | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `layoutRoot` | `boolean` | Make this element a layout root. Descendants' layout animations, including shared `layoutId` ones whose previous element was inside it too, resolve relative to it. Its own layout change is instant. Read at mount. |
| `layoutAnchor` | `{ x: number; y: number } \| false` | The point on the parent that relative layout projection is anchored to. Defaults to the top-left corner. `false` turns relative projection off. |

## Not yet supported

A shared `layoutId` crossfade with an element that is still exiting (an `exit` animation on the old underline) doesn't
follow the root yet: the exiting element stays where it was while the new one animates. Without `exit`, the handoff
works as described above.

Based on Motion's [`layoutRoot`](https://motion.dev/docs/react-layout-animations#layoutroot) and `layoutAnchor` props.
